WEBVTT
NOTE OpenIE Research TV v3.0 — mei (45s) Narrator Nylo

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.021
First, give a computer a fixed energy budget upfront,

2
00:00:04.021 --> 00:00:06.985
and get the best answer inside that limit.

3
00:00:07.425 --> 00:00:10.717
Second, a tiny food tag can refuse routine

4
00:00:10.717 --> 00:00:15.185
radio chatter in freezing cargo so safety alerts survive.

5
00:00:15.625 --> 00:00:22.534
Third, a data center can obey power-grid caps by pausing flexible work without giving wrong answers.

6
00:00:22.974 --> 00:00:27.134
Living cells throttle routine tasks the moment fuel runs low,

7
00:00:27.134 --> 00:00:35.591
whereas ordinary electronics transmit at full power until a freezing coin battery sags below two volts and dies mid-message.

8
00:00:36.031 --> 00:00:41.770
By reserving an energy slice before every action and sealing a signed receipt afterward,

9
00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:47.379
the same engineering discipline governs tiny cargo tags and grid-friendly AI campuses.
